# kevinpierik.dev

`kevinpierik.dev` is a one-page professional profile and curriculum vitae. The canonical public pages are `/` (English) and `/nl` (Dutch); unknown routes show a BIOS-style 404. There is no CMS: `app/[lang]/layout.tsx` renders the header, container, and footer, `app/[lang]/page.tsx` owns `<main>`, the metadata, and the structured data, `app/(default-locale)/` re-exports both for English at `/`, and the CV content is markdown in `app/[lang]/home.en.mdx` and `home.nl.mdx`.

How each feature works is documented for people in `docs/` ([index](docs/README.md)). Read the feature's page before changing it, and update it in the same pull request.

## Before you write code

- Next.js 16 may differ from what you know. Read the relevant guide in `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` first.
- For UI SDK code, read the `ui-sdk` skill and `node_modules/@ui-sdk/react/docs/llms.txt` first.
- Agent skills live in `.agents/skills/` (see [agent skills](docs/features/agent-skills.md)). Install or update them with `npx skills add`; never edit them by hand.

## Rules

- Every route stays static: `○`, or `●` for the prerendered `/[lang]` pages, in the build output. Route handlers export `dynamic = "force-static"`.
- Server Components are the default; add `"use client"` only for real client-side interaction.
- Keep components few. Hardcode content where it is used; extract only for real behaviour or reuse. Imports use `@/*`.
- Global styles are imported only through `app/shared-web-layout.tsx`. Use the `background` and `foreground` colours, never hardcoded black or white, and keep text contrast at 4.5:1 or more in both schemes ([color scheme](docs/features/color-scheme.md)).
- The homepage has one `<h1>`; CV sections use `<h2>` and entries `<h3>`. Never skip heading ranks or add deeper headings without real subsections.
- Keep landmarks, accessible names, visible focus styles, semantic lists, native links, and the reduced-motion rules in `features/style/animations.css`.
- Write CV content as plain markdown with the MDX components; no Tailwind classes in the MDX ([MDX content](docs/features/mdx-content.md)).
- Profile details are repeated in both `home.*.mdx` files, `app/[lang]/page.tsx` (metadata and structured data), the dictionaries in `features/i18n/`, `llms.txt`, and `app/manifest.ts`. Change them together ([SEO](docs/features/seo-and-structured-data.md)).
- Next.js merges `openGraph` shallowly, so declare the complete object.
- `robots.txt`, `sitemap.xml`, and `llms.txt` list only canonical public content; `/api/` stays disallowed.
- The BIOS 404 stays `noindex`. Only Enter, Escape, and F1 reboot it; never add page-wide character-key or pointer handlers (WCAG 2.1.4 and 2.5.2) ([BIOS 404](docs/features/bios-404.md)).

## Quality

Run `bun run qa` before considering work complete: it checks formatting, lints, type-checks, tests, and builds. Run `bun run format` before committing. Verify UI changes in a real browser with `bunx agent-browser` ([browser automation](docs/features/browser-automation.md)).

Tests use Vitest, live next to the code as `*.test.ts(x)`, and import their APIs from `vitest`. Test only real logic that is easy to break, not hardcoded content or framework behaviour.

## Conventions

- Write everything in English: commits, PRs, file names, code, UI copy, and documentation. Use kebab-case file names.
- Commits follow Zed's style: `area: Description`, imperative and sentence case, such as `bios: Use ui-sdk shortcuts for reboot`. Omit the area only for project-wide changes. No trailing period and no Co-authored-by trailers.
- `main` is the only long-lived branch. Work on a short kebab-case branch named after its area and squash-merge it through a pull request that follows `.github/pull_request_template.md` ([deployment](docs/deployment.md)).
- No explanatory comments in code; put lasting decisions in `docs/`.
- Local environment values go in `.env.local`; document them in `.env.example`.
- The MIT License covers code only; keep its personal-content exclusion.
- `.rules` is the single source of agent instructions; `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` are symlinks to it.

## Rules hygiene

Rules are traps to avoid, not maps to follow. The stack and folder layout are in the [README](README.md); how features work is in `docs/`.

- Do not edit `.rules` during normal feature or fix work. If you hit a trap a rule would have prevented, propose the rule in the pull request description instead.
- Add a rule only when an agent made, or would plausibly make, the same mistake again. One incident in unusual code is not enough.
- Do not put here what the code, the docs, or the tooling already enforce: project structure, feature explanations, formatting, or lint rules.
- When a rule no longer applies, remove it in the same pull request that changes the code.
